        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            The oil-based versus water-based question matters less than it used to. Most asphalt driveway sealers are already water-based emulsions, and modern water-based concrete sealers perform well in the hardest climates. Choose by surface first, then by weather and finish, and default to water-based when nothing points clearly the other way.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Keep solvent-based products for the jobs that genuinely need them: dried-out asphalt that needs rejuvenating, cool-season work where water-based products cannot cure, and decorative concrete where the deepest wet look is the goal. Whatever you choose, do not mix chemistries without a test patch, and respect the label's weather and safety requirements.
          </p>
